  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAn upper level ridge will build into the southeastern CONUS today \nand persist through Friday. This will allow for an influx of \nsouthern humidity and heat to move into the region as low level flow \nwith generally be southwest to westerly. 850 temperatures are \nexpected to climb in to the upper teens by Tuesday and peak around \n20-22C Wednesday. Surface temperatures as a result will start the \nweek in the mid to upper 80s today and climb into the low 90s for \nTuesday and Wednesday. Dew points will increase as well to the upper \n60s to low 70s resulting in heat indices in the upper 90s to low \n100s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nWith the upper level ridge in place, there will be multiple \nshortwaves moving along the northern periphery bringing energy to \nproduce rounds of thunderstorms through Friday. With the elevated \nheat and humidity in place over the region, this will help provide \nample instability to go along with northwesterly oriented bulk shear \nvectors of 30-40 knots. There's still some uncertainty in how the \namplified the ridge will be and locations of the shortwave vort \nmaxes as they move across the Great Lakes region. The highest \nconfidence for thunderstorm chances will be across NE OH and NW PA \nthroughout the week, though elsewhere will still have chances to see \nthunderstorms. This is outlined within the SPC's Day 2 severe \nweather outlook for Tuesday with a Marginal risk for the majority of \nthe CWA and a Slight risk for far eastern NW PA. This aligns well \nwith models location of the vort max moving through the Great Lakes \non Tuesday, though the better environment will be to the east in PA \nand NY. With the uncertainty mentioned above, will need to monitor \nhow this feature develops to have a better idea of impacts to the \nCWA. \n\nThe next round of thunderstorms will be late Wednesday night into \nThursday morning as a low pressure system moves through the Northern \nGreat Lakes. This will drag a cold front across the region and given \nthe timing of it being overnight, the severe weather potential will \nbe lower, though will not be zero. The upper level ridge will begin \nto retreat on Thursday into Friday to the west over the south-\ncentral CONUS. Thunderstorm and severe weather chances will decrease \ninto the weekend, though a few lingering showers and thunderstorms \nwill still be possible on Friday with a shortwave moving\nthrough.",
